How do you add built-in storage without making a room feel smaller? It can seem like adding more cupboards will automatically make a room feel crowded, particularly in smaller London homes. In reality, well-planned storage can have the opposite effect by reducing clutter and giving everything a defined place, especially when tailored through <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/bespoke-joinery-design-london/">bespoke joinery design London</a> specialists.

The key is to design storage around the room rather than simply adding another piece of furniture.
<h2>How Can Built-In Storage Make Better Use of a Small Room?</h2>
<img class="alignnone wp-image-11238 size-full" src="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Should-You-Get-Rid-of-Your-White-Kitchen.webp" alt="" width="1408" height="768" />
<h3>Where should built-in storage go?</h3>
Start by looking at the areas that are difficult to use with standard furniture. Alcoves, chimney breasts, corners and full-height walls can all provide opportunities for fitted storage.

Using the full height of a wall can be particularly effective. Instead of spreading several low cabinets around the room, one carefully designed unit can provide considerably more storage while keeping the floor relatively clear, as detailed in our guide on <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/designing-a-luxury-london-apartment-space-storage-architectural-detail/">designing a luxury London apartment with space and storage in mind</a>.
<h3>Should built-in storage match the walls?</h3>
It often helps.

Using a similar colour or finish to the surrounding walls can make cabinetry visually recede into the background. This can create a calmer appearance than using several contrasting pieces of furniture.

That doesn't mean everything has to be the same colour. Timber, fluted finishes or subtle detailing can add character while still keeping the overall effect cohesive, integrating architectural elements such as <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/wall-panelling-different-types-to-elevate-your-interiors/">wall panelling styles to elevate your interiors</a>.
<h3>Can storage be designed around awkward spaces?</h3>
This is where fitted storage has a major advantage over freestanding furniture.

An alcove that is too narrow for a standard wardrobe could become useful shelving or cabinetry. Similarly, storage can be shaped around sloping ceilings, structural features or unusual room proportions, which can be planned precisely using <a href="https://www.oraanj-interiors.co.uk/layout-plan-elevations-autocad-drawings/">architectural AutoCAD joinery drawings</a>.

